### Description
A named argument whose name is not a parameter name should go to the rest descriptor of an included record parameter. When that record type is an inclusive (open) record with an implicit `anydata...` rest descriptor, the compiler rejects the named argument with `no included record parameter accepts named argument 'count'`. The same program works when the rest descriptor is written out explicitly in an exclusive record (`record {| never name?; never extra?; int...; |}`), which prints `a {"count":3}`.

### Steps to Reproduce
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

type Extra record {
    never name?;
    never extra?;
};

function describe(string name, *Extra extra) returns string {
    return name + " " + extra.toString();
}

public function main() {
    io:println(describe("a", count = 3)); // @output a {"count":3}
    io:println(describe("b")); // @output b {}
}
```

Actual:
```
error[SEMANTIC_ERROR]: no included record parameter accepts named argument 'count'
  --> r2.bal:13:30
```
Expected: the program compiles and prints
```
a {"count":3}
b {}
```

Spec reasoning (2024R1):
- §5.4.2.2 (record types): "A type descriptor `record { F };` is thus equivalent to `record {| F; anydata...; |}`". So `Extra` has an implicit `anydata` rest descriptor.
- §6.15 (function call, named args): a named-arg may correspond to a field of an included-record-param that "is described by a record-rest-descriptor, either explicitly as part of an exclusive-record-type-descriptor or implicitly as part of an inclusive-record-type-descriptor". The conditions also hold:
  - Every parameter name (`name`, `extra`) has an optional `never` field.
  - There are no other individual fields.
  - There is no other included-record-param with a rest descriptor.
- `subset10.md` supports `included-record-param` and named arguments for callees with a known atomic signature. `describe` is a module-level function, so it qualifies.

### Affected Version(s)
main @ fd93c6a3f

### OS, DB, other environment details and versions
Ballerina dev (Nutcracker), built from `ballerina-nutcracker/ballerina` main @ fd93c6a3f, run with `go run ./cli/cmd run <file>.bal`. Linux.

### Related area
Type checker

### Related issue(s) (optional)
#488 (closed: exclusive record with explicit rest descriptor) and #1452 (an exclusive record that includes an open record loses the implicit `anydata...` rest). These are likely related.

Another case of the missing read-only bit, in the opposite direction. An object of a **mutable** class that has the same structure as a `readonly class` passes an `is` test against the readonly class type.

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

readonly class R {
    int x = 1;
}

class S {
    int x = 1;
}

public function main() {
    any s = new S();
    io:println(s is R); // @output false
}
```

Actual: `true`. Expected: `false`.

§8.6 (class definitions): "If class-type-quals contains readonly, then an object constructed using the class will have its read-only bit set ...; furthermore, an object shape belongs to the class's type only if its read-only bit is set." `new S()` does not have its read-only bit set, so it must not belong to `R`. Checked on main @ fd93c6a3f.

Update on main @ fd93c6a3f: the hex case no longer crashes with `INTERNAL_ERROR`. It now compiles and silently produces `-9223372036854775808`, which is not even the two's-complement value (`-1`). The same wrong value comes back when the literal has no `int` expected type (`var`, and an untyped `const`):

```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

const C = 9223372036854775808;

public function main() {
    int h = 0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF; // @error out-of-range int literal
    io:println(h);
    var v = 9223372036854775808; // @error out-of-range int literal
    io:println(v);
    io:println(C);
}
```

Actual output:
```
-9223372036854775808
-9223372036854775808
-9223372036854775808
```
Expected: a compile error for each out-of-range literal.
- §6.5: with no expected type, int is the first candidate selected for an int-literal.
- §5.2.3: int holds only -2^63 .. 2^63-1.

Two more symptoms of the same narrowing problem, with a `const int[] L = [1, 2];` pattern and a target of type `int[]|string`.

1. **Valid code is rejected.** Mutating the matched mutable list inside the clause fails with `incompatible type: expected 1, got 5`:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

const int[] L = [1, 2];

function f(int[]|string x) {
    match x {
        L => {
            x[0] = 5;
            io:println(x); // @output [5,2]
        }
        _ => {
            io:println("other");
        }
    }
}

public function main() {
    int[] a = [1, 2];
    f(a);
    io:println(a); // @output [5,2]
}
```
Expected: compiles and prints `[5,2]` twice.

2. **Unsound code is accepted.** The clause also lets a mutable value be bound to a readonly-typed variable. This compiles, and at runtime `r is readonly` prints `false`:
```ballerina
import ballerina/io;

const int[] L = [1, 2];

function f(int[]|string x) {
    match x {
        L => {
            int[] & readonly r = x; // @error x is a mutable int[] here
            io:println(r is readonly);
        }
        _ => {
        }
    }
}

public function main() {
    f([1, 2]);
}
```

Reasoning, from §7.21.2 and the definition of `&ro`: in the clause, `x` is narrowed to `T &ro L`. For the mutable list uniform type, `TU &ro SU` is `TU` when `TU & SU` is non-empty. So `x` should have type `int[]`: not readonly, and with no singleton member types. Checked on main @ fd93c6a3f.
